  "no_broadcast_scope": "For a unitary preserving each of two pure programs c,d uncorrelated while starting a target in the same pure input, inner-product conservation gives <c|d>=<c|d><out_c|out_d> (up to phase). Thus distinguishable pure outputs require orthogonal program states. Exactly distinct character programs are orthogonal: there is no such obstruction to their repeated controlled translations. For an arbitrary family of noncommuting programs one cannot infer a universal independent copy. A dephased orientation mixture may keep its own marginal exactly yet correlate all signals.",
  "bounds_and_costs": "For independently prepared real bases and individual clock-blind real records the good orientation laws coincide even for common adaptive feedback. If eta is the per-flight comparison error to a continuum law, using the same good clock for n independent ideal flights gives joint error <=e+n eta (or the sum of uniform conditional errors under feedback). Mean frequency error per flight is at most e+eta. A rare final herald E may increase the wrong posterior by up to e/P(E). Reuse lowers amortized setup only: an expected O(M r) preparation at tau per tick costs O(r/delta) mechanical time and O(r/(n delta)) per flight in the cited force scaling. The first usable clock still has its original causal latency. Correlated product-transcript estimates use e+n eta. To make both available upper bounds vanish one needs n delta/r -> infinity and n eta -> 0, feasible when r eta / delta -> 0. A convergence theorem with no quantitative rate does not give this tradeoff. Flights sharing a clock cannot be counted as independent calibrations. A translation of the support centre, even if record-dependent, does not change the sharp arc spectral bound. Sequential reuse assumes all flights can couple to this same physical register. Local travel, transport delay, and concurrent geographical copies require a separate physical account. This checker does not implement long-range couplings or new units."
